        {
          "say": "What is the difference between gross pay and net pay?",
          "reply": "**Gross pay** is the total amount you earn before anything is deducted. It's the starting figure for a pay period — built from your basic salary plus any allowances, overtime, or other earnings. **Net pay** is what actually reaches you after deductions are taken off. It's gross pay minus things like: - Income tax\n- Social insurance contributions\n- Any other statutory or agreed deductions\n- Loan installments, if you have an active loan So the relationship is simply: **Net pay = Gross pay − Total deductions** A couple of practical points: - Your payslip normally shows both, along with the itemised deductions in between, so you can trace exactly how gross became net.\n- Two people can have the same gross pay but different net pay, because deductions depend on individual circumstances.\n- When people talk about \"take-home pay,\" they mean net pay. If you'd like, I can walk you through how to read the earnings and deductions sections on your own payslip — just say the word.",
